    },
    "episode_id": "case-03d868b86683-0002",
    "evidence": "Invoice INV-2207 does not balance: the stated total due of 520 is not equal to the sum of its line items.\n\nLine-by-line check of the document as submitted:\n\n| Line | Item | Qty | Unit price | Qty x price | Stated line total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Pallet wrap | 3 | 120 | 360 | 360 (consistent) |\n| 2 | Corner boards | 2 | 45 | 90 | 90 (consistent) |\n\nSum of the line items: 360 + 90 = 450.\nStated total due in the invoice header: 520.\nDifference: 520 - 450 = 70.\n\nThe invoice itself records that \"No taxes, fees, or other lines appear on the invoice,\" so there is no additional documented line that could account for the 70-unit gap. The two line items are internally consistent (quantity x unit price matches each stated line total), and each stated line total also matches the line's own arithmetic.\n\nThe creator's claim is that \"the 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items.\" On the submitted record that equality does not hold: 520 is not equal to 450. The claim is therefore false as stated, and I dispute it.\n\nUncertainty I acknowledge: this establishes only that the header total and the line items are unequal. From the document alone I cannot tell whether the header total or one of the line items is the erroneous figure, nor whether an undocumented charge exists outside the invoice; no such charge is recorded on the invoice, and the claim is a comparison of the figures the invoice itself states. Nothing here is a judgment about the supplier's intent. I am not asking any juror to vote a particular way; each juror should verify the arithmetic against the document independently.\n",
    "evidence_hash": "0x78c8a2bc7a62de0ce26ee4cda078bab0108dcc7a6bcc5f1afc8db8c2f27ff04e",
