      "episode_id": "case-03d868b86683-0002",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Challenger note: I have challenged this claim. Invoice INV-2207 as submitted shows Pallet wrap 3 x 120 = 360 and Corner boards 2 x 45 = 90, so the line items sum to 450. The header states a total due of 520. The invoice itself says no taxes, fees, or other lines appear on it, so the 70-unit gap is not accounted for anywhere on the document. The claim that the 520 total equals the sum of the line items is therefore false as stated: 520 is not equal to 450. My full evidence is posted with the challenge. I am not asking any juror to vote a particular way -- please verify the arithmetic against the invoice yourself.",
          "content_hash": "0x14efa1f9b9969aad5d13448966a75bddbf3d52f8cf9a3eba3eb30f5bd518eee3",
          "created_at_ms": 1793230285592,
          "message_id": "089b0bd8-a406-4fbc-a7f1-c92d774b1e73",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 note on the record (case-0002, INV-2207). I checked the invoice arithmetic myself:\n- Line 1 Pallet wrap: 3 x 120 = 360, which matches the stated line total of 360.\n- Line 2 Corner boards: 2 x 45 = 90, which matches the stated line total of 90.\n- Sum of the line items: 360 + 90 = 450.\nThe invoice header states a total due of 520. The sum of the line items is 450, so the header total exceeds the sum of the line items by 70. The invoice also states that no taxes, fees, or other lines appear on it, so the 70 difference cannot be reconciled from any other line on the document.\n\nThe creator's claim is that 'the 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items.' On this record that claim is not correct: 520 does not equal 450. Each individual line total is correctly computed; the defect is in the header total, not in the line arithmetic. This is a statement about what the document shows, and I flag one limit honestly: I have only this invoice, so I cannot say why the header figure is 520 -- only that it does not match the stated line items.\n\nI am stating my own reading of the evidence and am not asking anyone to vote any particular way. Each juror and the parties should assess the record independently.",
          "content_hash": "0xe874bc6df7ba0da0b1bd2c20975703f452c759e0cfb61e80970e078d17eeea54",
          "created_at_ms": 1793230294160,
          "message_id": "801489b3-6e14-4ca0-beea-995483f4c221",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 independent assessment of invoice INV-2207.\n\nClaim under review: \"The 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items.\"\n\nArithmetic check of the submitted document:\n- Line 1, Pallet wrap: 3 units x 120 = 360. The stated line total of 360 is consistent.\n- Line 2, Corner boards: 2 units x 45 = 90. The stated line total of 90 is consistent.\n- Sum of the line totals: 360 + 90 = 450.\n- Header total due stated on the invoice: 520.\n- Difference: 520 - 450 = 70.\n\nThe invoice itself states that no taxes, fees, or other lines appear on it. On the face of the\ndocument, therefore, the 520 total due does not equal the sum of its line items (450); it exceeds\nthat sum by 70 units. The document contradicts the claim rather than supporting it.\n\nUncertainty I acknowledge: the invoice may omit a charge that should have been itemised, or the\nheader total may be a transcription error. Either possibility would explain the 70-unit gap, but\nneither rescues the claim as stated, because the claim asserts an equality between the printed\nheader total and the printed line items, and that equality fails on the document as submitted. I\nam not aware of any other evidence in this episode.\n\nConclusion: the claim is NOT supported. If the episode is put to a vote, my verdict is\nCHALLENGER_SUPPORTED.\n",
          "content_hash": "0x298c04491802e00093c0a3c28cdf1717ac6aa1bf3a39fea74e0fc2cc03529521",
          "created_at_ms": 1793230317277,
          "message_id": "2c3adc74-cd65-450b-955d-eb6e55592abc",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 independent assessment of INV-2207 (case-03d868b86683-0002), round 1.\n\nI verified the invoice arithmetic from the document itself:\n- Line 1 Pallet wrap: 3 x 120 = 360 (matches the stated line total of 360).\n- Line 2 Corner boards: 2 x 45 = 90 (matches the stated line total of 90).\n- Sum of line items: 360 + 90 = 450.\n- Header total due: 520. Difference: 520 - 450 = 70.\n- The invoice states no taxes, fees, or other lines appear on it, so no documented line accounts for the 70-unit gap.\n\nThe creator's claim is \"The 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items.\" 520 does not equal 450 on this record, so the claim is false as stated.\n\nUncertainty I acknowledge: this shows only that the header total and the line items are unequal. The document does not tell me which figure is wrong, nor whether an undocumented charge exists outside the invoice (none is recorded there). I make no claim about anyone's intent.\n\nConclusion: CHALLENGER_SUPPORTED. I am not asking any participant to vote a particular way; each juror should redo the arithmetic independently, and I am party to no vote-trading or coordination.",
          "content_hash": "0xf9d89fa34095ed34b03836971565d486222183fea9f5214c55b06590be1141ef",
          "created_at_ms": 1793230324370,
          "message_id": "2fb52958-bd3c-42fb-aa97-0d6463228a5a",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 4
        }
      ],
